\subsection*{Part A: Background measurement}
    \begin{steps}
        \newstep[] Obtain a Geiger counter. Turn it on and let it warm up for five minutes.
        \newstep[] Start the Geiger counter. Set up the measurement time to 60 seconds and the measuring voltage according to your professor's instructions. Mind selecting a voltage of 900V for all measurements (Press Display/High Voltage/Up/Down until you reach 900V). Press measure (press Display until the light cursor is next to count; then press Count until the stop button lights up.) and write down the background radioactivity in counts per minute in the table below.
        \newstep[] Measure the background radiation by reading the meter 10 times. 
          \newstep[]  Compute the average activity and standard deviation, s.
    \end{steps}     
       \subsection*{Part B: Calculation of $t_{1/2}$ for a set of mass measurements}
           \begin{steps}  
     \newstep[] Weight approximately 0.8 grams of KCl in a scale. Write down your measurement. Place the sample in the plastic tray which can inserted in the counter measuring rac.
    \newstep[] Place the measuring tray with the sample on the top insert, as close to the counter as possible. Be very careful with the membrane at the end of the counter, as it is very delicate and tears easily. Make sure to spread the KCl evenly over the entire bottom of the beaker.
            \newstep[] Set up the measurement time to 60 seconds and the measuring voltage according to your professor's instructions. Mind selecting a voltage of 900V for all measurements (Press Display/High Voltage/Up/Down until you reach 900V). Press measure (press Display until the light cursor is next to count; then press Count until the stop button lights up) and write down the activity in counts per minute in the Results section.
            \newstep[] Measure radiation by reading the meter 10 times. 
   %            \import{\labname/}{goodlabpractice}
        \newstep[] Repeat Part A for a set of K masses of 1g, 1.5g, and 2.0g by adding increasing amounts of KCl to the tray.
        \stepcounter{graphcounter}  
        \newstep[] For each sample, measure the activity 10 times. Write down the values in the Results section. Obtain the average $\overline{A}$ and stanrad deviation $s$ in cpm. 
        \newstep[] Compile all mass values ($m$) as well as average activity values ($\overline{A}$) in the results section. Compute log($\overline{A}$/m). Plot log($\overline{A}$/m) in the vertical axis versus $m$ in grams in the horizontal axis in Graph {\thegraphcounter}. The y-intercept will give you the highest $\overline{A}/m$ value.
         \newstep[] Transform the highest $\overline{A}$/m value into half-life and compare your measured value with the one reported for K-40.
             \end{steps}


