\vspace{0.5cm}\\
\mycircled{6} These are the activity per mass values in logarithmic form
\[  log(  \;\;\mycircled{5} \;\;\  / \;\;\mycircled{0} \;\;\       )\]
\mycircled{7} Use your graphing calculator or Excel to adjust a linear plot where Y would be log($\frac{A}{m}$) and X would be m. Obtain the y-intercept, the slope, and the $r^2$ value. \vspace{0.5cm}\\
\mycircled{8} Obtain the maximum value for $\frac{A}{m}$ by doing an antilogarithm of the Y-intercept
\[  Max(\frac{A}{m})=10^{Y-intercept}        \]
\vspace{0.5cm}\\
\mycircled{9} We can calculate now the half-life (in years) of the isotope
\[ t^{calc.}_{1/2} (LR)= \frac{0.693}{\;\;\mycircled{8} \;\;\ }\times   \frac{\epsilon}{100}\times\frac{9.53\times 10^{17}}{525600}\]
where $\epsilon$ is the efficiency of the Geiger counter (usually 20\%). The number 525600 is used to convert minutes to years, whereas the number $9.53\times 10^{17}\text{K-40 atoms}/\text{g KCl}$ is used to convert from K-40 atoms into KCl grams. \vspace{0.5cm}\\
\mycircled{10} This is the half-life of K-40 as reported in the literature\vspace{0.5cm}\\
\mycircled{11} This is the activity of the isotope\vspace{0.5cm}\\
\mycircled{12} This is the efficiency of the counter
\[ \epsilon= \frac{\;\;\mycircled{11} \;\;\ - \;\;\mycircled{3} \;\;\ }{A_{ref}\cdot 0.5^{ \frac{365\cdot t }{t_{\frac{1}{2}}} }     }  \times   \frac{100}{2.22\times 10^{6}}  \]
where $A_{ref}$ is the activity of the source in $\mu$Ci as listed on its label (0.1$\mu$ Ci for all samples), $t$ is the number of year passed from the date written in the chip, and $t_{\frac{1}{2}}$ is the half-life of the isotope. The number $2.22\times 10^{6}\text{cpm}/\mu Ci$ is used to convert from $\mu Ci$ to cpm  \vspace{0.5cm}\\
